AB  - In today's rapidly growing world of electric vehicles, people riding in them are more prone to fatalities due to delayed emergency response, and they are also misused, especially in remote areas. Current systems handle alcohol detection, accident alerts, and geofencing separately. This instance shows how important it is to have a combined solution. The proposed research paper presents an integrated safety system that combines real-time accident detection, alcoholbased ignition control, and geofencing features to bridge this gap. An MQ3 alcohol sensor in the developed prototype can identify alcohol in the driver's breath: the SW420 crash sensor and ADXL345 accelerometer work in tandem to detect collisions. The ESP32 microcontroller is used to process the sensor data. The Neo-7M Global Positioning System (GPS) module is used for location tracking, while the SIM900A Global System for Mobile Communications (GSM) contacts a predefined emergency contact in any emergency case. The Neo-7M GPS module logs vehicle data onto an SD card. Most of the current methods in this area only deal with alcohol detection or accident detection. The proposed system combines several safety features into a single platform, which solves several problems related to road safety.
ER  - 

