AB  - Rising fatalities in two-wheeler accidents, primarily due to alcohol-impaired driving and failure to wear helmets, underscore the need for an intelligent safety solution. The proposed work introduces a microcontroller-based smart system design to enforce rider safety through real-time condition monitoring and automated emergency response. The system utilizes multiple sensors to detect alcohol consumption (MQ-3), helmet usage (Infrared), rider vitals (pulse sensor), and crash events (MPU6050 and vibration sensors). An Arduino Uno acts as the processing unit, evaluating sensor data and controlling vehicle ignition accordingly. In critical scenarios, GPS coordinates are acquired using a NEO-6M module and transmitted to registered emergency contacts. Experimental trials indicate high detection accuracy with alcohol sensing values exceeding 800 units and reliable crash alert communication with minimal delay. The compact, low-power design makes it ideal for widespread deployment, offering a practical approach to enhancing two-wheeler road safety using embedded systems.
