AB  - Current vehicle safety systems usually function independently, focusing only on specific functions such as alcohol detection, health monitoring, and collision detection. This fragmented approach and reliance on human participation may cause emergency actions to be delayed, increasing the risk of fatalities. To solve these issues, this project Advanced Driver Health Monitoring and Vehicle Safety System that combines several safety components into a single IoT (Internet of Things) based platform. The technology tracks the driver’s use a pulse oximeter sensor to measure oxygen saturation and pulse rate in real time. The information is sent by the NodeMCU to the Blynk app. A gas sensor is used to measure alcohol consumption in order to ensure that the driver is not drunk. Accidents are detected by an accelerometer, and a panic button can be manually pressed by the driver to sound an alert in the event of discomfort or an emergency. In the event of anomalies like excessive alcohol intake, irregular heartbeats, or malfunctions, a GPS (Global Positioning System) and GSM (GSM Global System for Mobile Communications) module instantly notifies pre-designated emergency contacts of the driver’s current location. The Arduino UNO’s interfaces utilizing the alcohol sensor, panic button, and accelerometer, while the NodeMCU manages the real-time health data transfer. A DC motor is included to simulate vehicle movement and control under test conditions. By automating notifications and guaranteeing prompt emergency response, this intelligent, real-time technology improves road safety. The system offers a proactive strategy for secure and secure driving by integrating location tracking, alcohol detection, health monitoring, accident detection, and cloud communication.
