AB  - Dense smoke and haze from indoor fire situations significantly reduce visibility and make rescue efforts more difficult. Convolutional neural network techniques, polarization-based techniques, and physics-based scattering models have all been investigated in earlier de-smoking and dehazing research. These approaches have demonstrated good accuracy but have trouble with changing smoke densities, dynamic lighting, and real-time processing demands. In order to capture both local detail and global context, this study offers an AI-ML powered de-smoking/de-hazing system that integrates a U-Net Convolutional Neural Network backbone with a Feature Focus Attention Network and Dehamer transformer encoder. The model used a composite loss for improved perceptual quality after being trained on a hybrid dataset that included geolocation-tagged data, public smoke/haze imagery, and controlled fire situations. The proposed system, provides real-time, smoke-free, high-definition video feeds with low latency. The system performs exceptionally well when evaluated using PSNR and SSIM metrics, which makes it ideal for use in urgent fire rescue situations.
ER  - 

